# The human as a tool -- which is the whole reason this harness exists.
#
# UGM can register an *answerer*: something that answers a request without
# searching for it. HarneSkills registers YOU as one. The corpus asks; the TUI
# stops and prompts; what you type comes back as an answer the agent can reason
# from -- and, crucially, can decline to believe.
#
#   /load corpus/ask.ugm
#   /run                      -- it will stop and ask you
#   (type e.g.  sunny(here)  )
#   /why sunny(here)
#
# ⚠ Answer with a TERM, not prose: `sunny(here)`, not "it's sunny out". An
# unparseable answer is read as *nothing to say*, which is a real answer and not
# an error -- a tool that must answer everything is a tool nothing can decline.

# Anything wanted that we have no plan for is worth asking about. In a real
# deployment this would be narrower; here it is deliberately eager so that one
# `/run` reaches the prompt.
rule <curious> = implies(
    { +goal(?w) },
    { +ask(?w) } )

# ⭐ What comes back is `answered(<human>, question, answer)` -- a record that the
# human said so, not a belief. Exactly the treatment an arrival on a channel
# gets, and for the same reason: a confident tool must not be able to launder a
# guess into a fact. THIS rule is what turns the record into a claim, and it is
# the one to delete if you want an agent that consults you and then decides for
# itself. Wrap it -- `+likely(?a)` -- for an agent that takes your word as a
# hint rather than as knowledge.
rule <believe-human> = implies(
    { +answered(<human>, ?q, ?a) },
    { +?a } )

# What is wanted. It names no plan, so the only way to it is to ask.
fact +goal(weather(today))
